# Per-instance configuration for hft-collector@<instance>.service.
#
# Copy to /opt/hft-collector/etc/<instance>.env, one file per collection job.
# The instance name is the file's basename:
#
#   cp instance.env.example /opt/hft-collector/etc/bybit.env
#   $EDITOR /opt/hft-collector/etc/bybit.env
#   sudo systemctl enable --now hft-collector@bybit
#
# This file is sourced by systemd's EnvironmentFile, NOT by a shell. That
# means: no command substitution, no variable expansion, no inline comments
# after a value. Quotes around a value are stripped by systemd.

# Which venue to record. One of:
#   binancefutures | binancefuturesum   USD-M perpetual futures
#   binancefuturescm                    COIN-M futures
#   binance | binancespot               spot
#   bybit                               linear perpetuals (V5)
#   hyperliquid                         perpetuals
# The stream/topic set per venue is fixed in collector/src/main.rs.
COLLECTOR_EXCHANGE=hyperliquid

# Whitespace-separated symbols, in the venue's own notation, passed through
# verbatim — case matters. Output filenames are always lowercased regardless.
#
# Hyperliquid perps have NO quote suffix: `BTC`, not `BTCUSDT`. The quote shown
# in the web UI is the dex's collateral token, not part of the instrument name.
#
# HIP-3 builder dexes each have their own universe and their own collateral,
# and their names carry a `dex:` prefix which IS the wire name — `hyna:ENA`
# (USDE-collateralised) is a different instrument from the canonical `ENA`
# (USDC), with a separate book. `GOLD` exists ONLY on builder dexes, so one has
# to be chosen deliberately; `xyz:GOLD` is USDC-collateralised.
#
# On Hyperliquid, Paradex, Extended and Lighter every name is verified against
# the venue at startup and the collector refuses to run on an unknown one — on
# Hyperliquid a single bad coin closes the whole WebSocket and takes the valid
# subscriptions down with it.
#
# On BINANCE AND BYBIT IT IS NOT. Those venues subscribe through a URL (or ack a
# batch and then say nothing), so a misspelled symbol is accepted, acked and
# served zero frames for ever — measured 2026-08-27: `btcusdcc` alongside two
# real symbols produced no error anywhere, no file, and no complaint from the
# venue. What catches it instead, in this order:
#   * no `<symbol>_<day>.gz` is ever created — visible in the first `ls`;
#   * COLLECTOR_LIVENESS_TIMEOUT_S: one WARN per minute past the threshold,
#     naming the symbol, plus a `liveness` record in `_meta`;
#   * COLLECTOR_STALL_TIMEOUT_MIN, if EVERY symbol is wrong: fatal exit, alert;
#   * the nightly gate: `missing_required` = RED the next morning.
# So a typo costs up to a day of one symbol. Check the first `ls` of the data
# directory against this list; do not rely on a startup refusal that does not
# exist for these venues.
#
# Bybit example:   COLLECTOR_SYMBOLS=BTCUSDT ETHUSDT
# Binance topics are lowercase: btcusdt
COLLECTOR_SYMBOLS=SUI SEI TIA INJ JTO PENDLE ENA ONDO HYPE xyz:GOLD

# Where the .gz files land. One file per symbol per UTC day:
#   ${COLLECTOR_DATA_DIR}/<symbol-lowercase>_<YYYYMMDD>.gz
#
# Optional. Left out, it defaults to /opt/hft-collector/data/<instance>, which
# is the safe choice: two collectors may NOT share a directory, and the binary
# enforces it with an exclusive flock on <dir>/.collector.lock — the second one
# exits non-zero naming the instance and pid that holds it. Sharing would
# interleave gzip members from two processes into one unreadable file, so if
# you do set this, set it to something no other instance uses.
#
# This grows without bound and is never pruned by the collector — budget disk
# and add your own retention. If you point this outside
# /opt/hft-collector/data you MUST also widen ReadWritePaths in a unit
# drop-in, or the service will fail to write under ProtectSystem=strict.
#COLLECTOR_DATA_DIR=/opt/hft-collector/data/hyperliquid

# Log verbosity (tracing EnvFilter syntax). `info` gives connect/disconnect,
# date rotation and startup provenance. `debug` is very noisy at tick rates.
RUST_LOG=info

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- optional
# Everything below is optional. Leave a line out and the binary's own default
# applies — the wrapper omits the flag entirely rather than restating it.

# Stop and exit non-zero when free space on COLLECTOR_DATA_DIR's filesystem
# falls below this. Checked at startup and every minute. Default 5, `0`
# disables. Budget from the measured rates: a Hyperliquid major is ~22 MB/day,
# a Bybit linear pair with depths 1,50 is ~86 MB/day — several times that in a
# volatile session.
#COLLECTOR_MIN_FREE_GB=5

# Stop and exit non-zero when NO market data at all has been written for this
# many minutes. Default 5, `0` disables. It catches the failures nothing else
# reports — a subscription the venue accepted and never feeds, a reconnect loop
# that never resubscribes — where the process otherwise looks perfectly healthy.
#
# 5 is a proposal, not a measurement. The slowest legitimate feed is
# Hyperliquid's plain l2Book at ~5.4s, so the margin is roughly fiftyfold.
# It only ever fires on TOTAL silence: a dead depth stream with trades still
# arriving passes it unnoticed. One symbol of ten going quiet is the next
# variable, not this one.
#COLLECTOR_STALL_TIMEOUT_MIN=5

# WARN (never stop) when a single symbol has recorded nothing for this many
# seconds. `0` disables the warning but keeps the minutely `liveness` gauge in
# _meta. Leave the line out and the venue's own default applies: 60 where a
# periodic ~1/s per-symbol feed exists (hyperliquid `activeAssetCtx`,
# binancefuturescm `@markPrice@1s`), 300 elsewhere, where order flow is the
# only feed and a thin symbol is legitimately quiet.
#
# This is the per-symbol half of COLLECTOR_STALL_TIMEOUT_MIN, which cannot see
# nine symbols arriving and one silent. It warns rather than stopping because
# partial silence is ambiguous where total silence is not. Raise it for an
# instance recording thin symbols rather than learning to ignore the warning.
#COLLECTOR_LIVENESS_TIMEOUT_S=60

# Bybit only. Orderbook depths to subscribe to. A single rejected topic fails
# the whole batch, and `500` is rejected for the majors despite being
# documented. Default 1,50; 200 is a safe widening for BTCUSDT/ETHUSDT/SOLUSDT.
#COLLECTOR_BYBIT_DEPTHS=1,50

# Hyperliquid only. Which l2Book cadences to record. `slow` = 20 levels/~5.4s,
# `fast` = 5 levels/~0.54s, `none` = no book. Default records both, so that
# different merge policies can be compared over the same recording later.
#COLLECTOR_HL_L2_MODES=slow,fast

# Set to 1 to skip the startup check that every symbol exists on the venue.
# Only do this if the venue's symbol list endpoint is unreachable — on
# Hyperliquid an unknown coin closes the whole WebSocket and the collector
# then records partial data indefinitely while appearing healthy.
#COLLECTOR_NO_SYMBOL_CHECK=0

# Which dataset profile the NIGHTLY GATE judges THIS instance against.
# `gate-run.sh` reads this variable out of this file (without executing it);
# the collector itself never looks at it.
#
#   mode-a-v1  (default) Hyperliquid is the traded venue, Binance is the
#              signal, and `@bookTicker` is the only Binance stream mode A
#              reads. Losing `@depth@0ms` or `@trade` is yellow.
#   book-v1    the same, except the Binance families must carry the whole order
#              flow: bookTicker, trade AND depthUpdate, all three required.
#              For an instance recorded BECAUSE the venue publishes no book
#              anywhere else — Binance USD-M has no `bookTicker` archive after
#              2024-04 and none at all for a perpetual listed later — where
#              losing the book is not a downgrade, it is the recording failing
#              at the one thing it exists for. The timer escalates on red only,
#              so under mode-a-v1 that loss would page nobody.
#GATE_PROFILE=mode-a-v1
